// Editor-side companion to this extension.
//
// The extension itself only colors [oz[ ... ]oz] in the Markdown PREVIEW.
// This block colors the same markers in the Markdown SOURCE, which is where
// the replies actually get typed. It needs the Highlight extension:
//
//     code --install-extension fabiospampinato.vscode-highlight
//
// Paste into VS Code user settings (Ctrl+Shift+P -> "Preferences: Open User
// Settings (JSON)"). Already installed on Oz's machine as of 2026-08-19.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// About the convention
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Oz reviews long documents by answering inline, in the same file, wrapping his
// replies in [oz[ ... ]oz]. Several Claude Code skills depend on this — most
// notably /extensive-feedback, which runs a multi-round file-based Q&A where
// Claude writes numbered questions into a scratch doc and Oz answers under each
// one inside these markers. The markers are asymmetric so they stay greppable
// and never collide with real Markdown syntax.
//
// The three `decorations` entries map to the three capture groups: opening
// marker, reply body, closing marker. Markers are dimmed so the eye lands on
// the reply text rather than on the punctuation.
//
// `[\s\S]*?` rather than `.*?` so a reply spanning several lines still matches;
// lazy so two adjacent replies don't merge into one run.

{
  "highlight.regexFlags": "gi",
  "highlight.regexes": {
    "(\\[oz\\[)([\\s\\S]*?)(\\]oz\\])": {
      "filterLanguageRegex": "markdown",
      "decorations": [
        { "color": "#f0a55899", "fontWeight": "bold" },
        { "color": "#f0a558" },
        { "color": "#f0a55899", "fontWeight": "bold" }
      ]
    }
  }
}
